PLACES=[
N('castillo-de-san-marcos','Castillo de San Marcos','St. Augustine','fl','fort','standing',29.8979,-81.31174,'exact','open','walk','forts','1672–1695',
  'A coquina star fort on the shore of Matanzas Bay, begun in 1672 after an English privateer\'s raid of 1668 burned much of the town and its wooden fort; the core was finished in 1695. Its shell-rock walls absorbed English cannon fire in Oglethorpe\'s siege of 1740. The British called it Fort St. Mark from 1763 to 1783, and the United States, which took Florida in 1821, renamed it Fort Marion and used it as a military prison: Osceola and other Seminoles were held there in 1837, when Coacoochee and nineteen others escaped through a cell window, and Plains and Apache prisoners from 1875.',
  'The four bastions, the ravelin before the sally port, the dry moat, the gun deck and the vaulted casemates that served as storerooms and later as cells. Part of the moat was filled in the American period for a water battery with a hot-shot furnace. The coquina was quarried on Anastasia Island across the bay (see the Spanish Coquina Quarries).',
  'The oldest masonry fort in the continental United States, built of shells.',
  'Castillo de San Marcos National Monument (National Park Service). Open every day except Thanksgiving and Christmas: first admission 9:00 a.m., last 5:00 p.m., visitors out by 5:15 p.m.; the grounds close from midnight to 6 a.m., and the gun deck closes in thunderstorms (NPS hours page). An entrance pass is required (NPS fees page). The city keeps a parking lot in front of the fort for vehicles 21 feet or shorter, and a garage off West Castillo Drive (NPS directions page).',
  [W('Castillo de San Marcos'),['NPS: Castillo de San Marcos hours','https://www.nps.gov/casa/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],['NPS: Castillo de San Marcos fees','https://www.nps.gov/casa/planyourvisit/fees.htm'],['NPS: Castillo de San Marcos directions','https://www.nps.gov/casa/planyourvisit/directions.htm']],hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5:15 p.m., last entry 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving and Christmas',brick='no'),
N('fort-matanzas','Fort Matanzas','Summer Haven','fl','fort','standing',29.71524,-81.23902,'exact','open','boat','forts','1740–1742',
  'A small coquina watchtower fort on Rattlesnake Island guarding Matanzas Inlet, the back door to St. Augustine. Oglethorpe of Georgia used the inlet in his 1740 siege, so the Spanish built the tower from 1740 to 1742 on pine pilings driven into the marsh, with convicts, slaves and troops from Cuba as labor. Its guns drove off British scouting boats in 1742. The United States never used it and it fell into ruin; the War Department repaired its cracked walls from 1916, and it became a national monument in 1924.',
  'A coquina blockhouse about 50 feet on a side with a 30-foot tower, reached by a free five-minute ferry from the visitor center on Anastasia Island. The visitor center itself is a two-storey building with an arched breezeway, listed on the Register on its own.',
  'A Spanish tower in the marsh, reached only by a five-minute ferry.',
  'Fort Matanzas National Monument (National Park Service), on A1A about 15 miles south of St. Augustine. The park is open 9:00 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. every day except Thanksgiving and Christmas. The free ferry to the fort leaves the visitor-center dock and does not run on Tuesdays or Wednesdays; every rider, babies included, needs a free boarding pass from the visitor center, and passes are often all gone before 11 a.m. No pets (except service animals) and no food or drink on the boat or at the fort; trips stop for lightning and wind (NPS hours page). The pin is the fort; park at the visitor center on A1A (the mapped lot could not be picked out on the aerial).',
  [W('Fort Matanzas National Monument'),['NPS: Fort Matanzas hours and ferry','https://www.nps.gov/foma/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5:30 p.m.; ferry to the fort Thursday to Monday only; closed Thanksgiving and Christmas',brick='no'),
N('spanish-coquina-quarries','Spanish Coquina Quarries (King\'s Quarry)','St. Augustine Beach','fl','industry','ruins',29.8797,-81.2852,'site','public','walk','','1671–',
  'The shell-rock pits on Anastasia Island that gave the stone for the Castillo de San Marcos and much of colonial St. Augustine. Large-scale quarrying began in 1671. Native American workers stripped the soil; stonecutters chipped grooves, drove in wedges and snapped blocks free with crowbars, and the blocks dried for up to a year before use. Later the workers were mostly enslaved Africans, convicts and captives. Oxcarts hauled the blocks to the river, where barges carried them across the bay. The site was listed on the National Register in 1972 and no more stone is taken.',
  'A short trail from the Spanish Coquina Quarries trailhead on Anastasia Park Road with interpretive markers on the cutting and the tools. The marker on the quarry pit says trees have grown up round its rim and some have fallen in, and the floor is wet sediment except in drought. The St. Augustine Amphitheatre next door is built on part of the royal quarry.',
  'The hole the Castillo came out of.',
  'Anastasia State Park (Florida State Parks). The park is open 8 a.m. until sunset every day of the year and charges an entrance fee (park brochure); the visitor bureau gives $8 per vehicle, $4 for one person and $2 on foot or bicycle (not read on the state\'s own fee page, which refused the tool). The trailhead is on Anastasia Park Road about 0.1 mile east of A1A (HMdb).',
  [NR('72001462','Spanish Coquina Quarries'),['Florida State Parks: Anastasia State Park brochure','https://www.floridastateparks.org/sites/default/files/media/file/Anastasia_State_Park_Brochure_PRESS.pdf'],HM(153682,'Spanish Mining of the Quarry'),HM(256039,'Changing Times in the Quarry'),HM(28361,'Old Spanish Quarries'),['Florida\'s Historic Coast: Anastasia State Park','https://www.floridashistoriccoast.com/beaches/anastasia-state-park']],hours='Daily 8 a.m. to sunset',brick='no'),
N('old-spanish-chimney-and-well-anastasia','Old Spanish Chimney and Well','St. Augustine','fl','industry','ruins',29.86457,-81.28043,'area','view','drive','','1600s',
  'Ruins that the county historical commission\'s 1975 marker calls all that is left of what was probably a Spanish barracks for the quarry overseer, master masons and stonecutters who cut coquina for the Castillo de San Marcos. The quarry lay across the road. The marker names the overseer as Alonso Díaz Mejiá.',
  'A chimney and a well, by the marker at the corner of Old Beach Road and Riviera Street (HMdb). What they are built of and how much stands was not checked.',
  '',
  'Ownership not checked: view from Old Beach Road. Rests on the one county marker; no other source was found.',
  [HM(106439,'Old Spanish Chimney and Well')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('gonzalez-alvarez-house','González–Álvarez House (the Oldest House)','St. Augustine','fl','landmark','standing',29.88815,-81.31021,'exact','open','walk','','c. 1723',
  'Thought to be the oldest surviving house in St. Augustine. The first floor was built about 1723 and was lived in by Tomás González y Hernández, an artilleryman at the Castillo; English owners later added the wooden upper storey. A building stood on the lot already in the 1600s. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1970 and is owned by the St. Augustine Historical Society.',
  'A two-storey house with a coquina ground floor, a clapboarded wooden upper floor and a hip roof of wooden shingles, showing changes from the Spanish, British and American periods. Open by guided tour as part of the Oldest House Museum Complex. Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'St. Augustine Historical Society, 14 St. Francis Street. Open daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. with guided tours every half hour; admission charged (St. Johns County visitor bureau listing; the society\'s own page refused the tool). OpenStreetMap maps an Oldest House parking lot on Charlotte Street beside it.',
  [W('González–Álvarez House'),VB('oldest-house-museum-complex','Oldest House Museum Complex')],hours='?',brick='no'),
N('ximenez-fatio-house','Ximenez-Fatio House','St. Augustine','fl','landmark','standing',29.89108,-81.31177,'exact','open','walk','','1797–1802',
  'A two-storey house built by the Spanish-born merchant Andres Ximenez in the Second Spanish Period (the article gives 1797–1802), with his family upstairs and his business below. After Florida became American it was a boarding house for the first winter visitors, run by women and last by Louisa Fatio. The National Society of the Colonial Dames of America in Florida has owned it since 1939.',
  'One of the best-preserved Second Spanish Period houses in the city, on Aviles Street, the oldest archaeologically documented street in the country. The original detached kitchen with its beehive oven stands beside the main house. Furnished as an 1830s–1850s boarding house.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Colonial Dames of Florida, 20 Aviles Street. Open for tours Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., last tour start 4 p.m., docent tours at 11, 1 and 3; admission charged (St. Johns County visitor bureau listing; the museum\'s own page refused the tool). '+TOWN,
  [W('Ximenez-Fatio House'),VB('ximenez-fatio-house-museum','Ximenez-Fatio House Museum')],hours='?',brick='?'),
N('cathedral-basilica-st-augustine','Cathedral Basilica of St. Augustine','St. Augustine','fl','worship','standing',29.89307,-81.3124,'exact','open','walk','','1793–1797',
  'The seat of the Catholic bishop of St. Augustine, whose parish was founded in 1565. The present church was built from 1793 to 1797 of coquina. It burned on April 12, 1887, but the coquina walls survived; James Renwick rebuilt it with an exposed timber roof and added the bell tower, which is cast concrete. A National Historic Landmark since 1970.',
  'A Spanish mission front with curved bell gables, a statue niche and a Doric doorway under a broken pediment, the 1797 coquina walls, Renwick\'s decorated roof timbers, and four bells in the tower, one saved from an earlier church. Interior fabric beyond that not checked.',
  '',
  'The cathedral parish, 38 Cathedral Place on the Plaza. Usually open Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Masses daily (cathedral\'s page). '+TOWN,
  [W('Cathedral Basilica of St. Augustine'),['Cathedral Basilica parish page (hours and Masses)','https://www.thefirstparish.org/']],hours='Usually Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Mass Monday to Saturday 7 a.m., Sunday 7, 9 and 11 a.m. and 5 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('mission-nombre-de-dios-chapel','Mission Nombre de Dios and the Chapel of Our Lady of La Leche','St. Augustine','fl','worship','standing',29.90508,-81.31606,'site','open','walk','','1587',
  'The Franciscan mission founded in 1587 at the landing place of Menéndez\'s settlers in 1565, where the expedition\'s chaplain said a Mass of thanksgiving. It served the Mocama Timucua village of Nombre de Dios. The shrine chapel was destroyed in the British siege of 1728, rebuilt in 1875 and damaged by a hurricane; the present small coquina chapel replaced it (Wikipedia gives 1914, the visitor bureau 1915).',
  'The ivy-covered coquina chapel, seating about 30, with a large stained-glass window of the Holy Spirit; the Rustic Altar, the Sacred Acre cemetery, a museum holding coquina from the original chapel foundation, and the 208-foot stainless-steel Great Cross by the marsh.',
  'The landing place of 1565, with a chapel rebuilt in coquina.',
  'Catholic Diocese of St. Augustine. The grounds are open dawn to dusk every day; the historic chapel and shrine church Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; the museum charges no admission (visitor bureau listing; Wikipedia for the museum). Free parking for visitors who come for religious purposes, in the mission lot off Ocean Avenue.',
  [W('Mission Nombre de Dios'),VB('mission-nombre-de-dios','Mission Nombre de Dios'),['Mission and Shrine: pilgrimage planning guide','https://missionandshrine.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Pilgrimage-Planning-Guide.pdf']],th=[29.90471,-81.3171],thn='Mission Nombre de Dios lot (OpenStreetMap)',conf='mixed',hours='?',brick='no'),
N('tolomato-cemetery','Tolomato Cemetery','St. Augustine','fl','burial','standing',29.89699,-81.31521,'site','open','walk','cemeteries','1700s–1884',
  'The site of Tolomato, a village of Guale converts and their Franciscan friars shown on a 1737 map. The British pulled down its wooden church for firewood after 1763, leaving the coquina bell tower. Menorcan refugees from New Smyrna buried their dead here from 1777, and it was the cathedral parish\'s cemetery from 1784 until all cemeteries in the city were closed in 1884. Bishop Augustin Verot (died 1876) lies in the mortuary chapel at the back.',
  'Graves and tombs behind the gate on Cordova Street, and the mortuary chapel at the back. Whether anything of the bell tower survives was not checked.',
  '',
  'Open to the public only on the third Saturday of each month, 11 a.m. to 2 p.m., with free self-guided and docent tours; come to the gate on Cordova Street (Tolomato Cemetery preservation page). Otherwise view through the gate. '+TOWN,
  [W('Tolomato Cemetery'),['Tolomato Cemetery (open days)','https://www.tolomatocemetery.com/']],hours='Third Saturday of each month, 11 a.m. to 2 p.m.',brick='?'),
N('huguenot-cemetery-st-augustine','Huguenot Cemetery (Public Burying Ground)','St. Augustine','fl','burial','standing',29.89829,-81.3142,'site','open','walk','cemeteries','1821–1884',
  'The Protestant burying ground opened just outside the City Gate when the Americans took over in 1821, since the only cemetery in town, Tolomato, was for Catholics. The first burials came just before a yellow fever epidemic. The Presbyterian church took title in 1832, and burials stopped in 1884. City records give at least 436 burials. Despite the name, no Huguenots are known to lie here.',
  'Marble head and foot stones, slabs on brick foundations, coquina markers and brick-walled family plots, across from the City Gate.',
  '',
  'Opened to the public on the third Saturday of each month from 11 a.m. (St. Johns County visitor bureau listing); otherwise view through the iron gate. '+TOWN,
  [W('Huguenot Cemetery'),VB('huguenot-cemetery','Huguenot Cemetery'),NR('100006756','St. Augustine Public Burying Ground')],hours='?',brick='?'),
N('ponce-de-leon-hotel-flagler-college','Hotel Ponce de Leon (Flagler College)','St. Augustine','fl','hotel','standing',29.892222,-81.315,'site','open','walk','hotels','1885–1888',
  'Henry Flagler\'s winter resort hotel, built from 1885 to 1887 and opened in January 1888: the first big work of Carrère & Hastings, poured in concrete with coquina as aggregate, and wired for electricity from the start with Edison generators. Rail extension south drew the rich away in the following decades. Since 1968 it has been the centre of Flagler College.',
  'Spanish Renaissance concrete walls trimmed with burnt-orange and red brick string courses, window surrounds and quoins, and brick gate columns with terra-cotta lions (Register form, 1975). Inside, the dining room keeps Tiffany stained glass and the rotunda and dining room George Maynard\'s murals (Wikipedia). Student-led tours cover the ground-floor public rooms, the rotunda and courtyard, not the dormitories.',
  'Flagler\'s poured-concrete palace, now a college dining hall under Tiffany glass.',
  'Flagler College; an active campus. Historic tours run daily, tickets through the college\'s tour page (hours there not read in full). '+TOWN,
  [W('Ponce de Leon Hotel'),NR('75002067','Hotel Ponce de Leon'),['Flagler College: Historic Tours','https://legacy.flagler.edu/pages/tours']],hours='Guided tours daily; times on the college\'s tour page',brick='some'),
N('alcazar-hotel-lightner-museum','Hotel Alcazar (Lightner Museum)','St. Augustine','fl','hotel','standing',29.891667,-81.314167,'site','open','walk','hotels','1887–1932',
  'Flagler\'s second hotel, opposite the Ponce de Leon, designed by Carrère & Hastings in poured concrete in the Spanish Renaissance style for guests of the Florida East Coast Railway. It had steam and sulphur baths, a gymnasium, a three-storey ballroom and what was then the largest indoor swimming pool in the world. It closed in 1932; Otto Lightner bought it in 1947 for his collections and the museum opened in 1948.',
  'The museum fills three floors of the old spa, Turkish bath and ballroom; from the ballroom\'s upper balcony you look down on the drained swimming pool.',
  'Look down into the drained pool of a Gilded Age spa hotel.',
  'Lightner Museum, owned by the City of St. Augustine (Wikipedia). Open daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., upper floors close at 4:30; closed Easter Sunday, Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day; admission charged (museum\'s page). '+TOWN,
  [W('Lightner Museum'),['Lightner Museum: hours and admission','https://lightnermuseum.org/hours-admission/']],hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., upper floors to 4:30; closed Easter, Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day',brick='?'),
N('villa-zorayda','Villa Zorayda','St. Augustine','fl','landmark','standing',29.8916,-81.31511,'exact','open','walk','','1883',
  'The Boston businessman Franklin W. Smith\'s winter house of 1883, inspired by the Alhambra in Granada and the first Moorish Revival house in Florida. It was the first poured-concrete building in St. Augustine, cast in layers with crushed coquina, and the method was taken up by Flagler for his hotels. The Mussallem family bought it with part of its collection in 1913 and has run it as a museum since 1933.',
  'Moorish concrete walls and courtyard, holding the art and antiques collected by Smith and Abraham Mussallem. Self-guided audio tours in English, Spanish and French.',
  '',
  'Villa Zorayda Museum, privately owned by the Mussallem family, 83 King Street. Open Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Sunday from 11 a.m., last admission 30 minutes before closing; admission charged (visitor bureau listing). '+TOWN,
  [W('Villa Zorayda'),VB('villa-zorayda-museum','Villa Zorayda Museum'),['Villa Zorayda Museum','https://villazorayda.com/']],hours='?',brick='no'),
N('old-st-johns-county-jail','Old St. Johns County Jail','St. Augustine','fl','inst','standing',29.90785,-81.31854,'exact','open','walk','','1891–1953',
  'A county jail of 1891 by the P. J. Pauley Jail Building Company of St. Louis, paid for by Henry Flagler, who wanted the old jail\'s land for the Ponce de Leon Hotel. It held up to 72 prisoners, with a maximum-security block and a death-row cell, and eight men were hanged in its yard. It closed in 1953 and has been a roadside attraction since.',
  'Load-bearing brick walls finished in smooth stucco, four corbelled brick chimneys on the jail wing (Register form), the cells and the sheriff\'s living quarters.',
  '',
  'A private museum run by Old Town Trolley Tours, 167 San Marco Avenue: entry by guided tour only, daily 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., tours every 15 minutes; shorter hours on Easter, Thanksgiving and Christmas Eve; admission charged (operator\'s page). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Old St. Johns County Jail'),NR('87001427','Old St. Johns County Jail'),['Old Town Trolley Tours: Old Jail','https://trolleytours.com/st-augustine/old-jail']],hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., guided tours every 15 minutes',brick='no'),
N('st-augustine-light','St. Augustine Light Station','St. Augustine','fl','light','standing',29.88545,-81.28854,'exact','open','drive','','1871–1874',
  'Florida\'s first American lighthouse was lit here in 1824 on or near the site of Spanish and British watchtowers; the present tower was built from 1871 to 1874 and lit that October with a first-order Fresnel lens. It is still a working aid to navigation. A not-for-profit museum owns the tower, the original lens and the grounds.',
  'A brick tower painted in black and white spiral bands, with a red ball finial on the lantern, a brick entrance building at its base, and the keeper\'s house, built of brick on a coquina foundation as a two-family house (Register form). The original first-order lens is still in the lantern.',
  '',
  'St. Augustine Lighthouse & Maritime Museum, a non-profit, on Red Cox Drive, Anastasia Island. Open daily 9 a.m. to 6 p.m.; admission charged (museum\'s page). Parking not checked; the museum\'s page mentions free parking.',
  [W('St. Augustine Light'),NR('81000668','St. Augustine Lighthouse and Keeper\'s Quarters'),['St. Augustine Lighthouse & Maritime Museum','https://www.staugustinelighthouse.org/']],hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 6 p.m.',brick='no'),
N('fort-mose','Fort Mose (Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose)','St. Augustine','fl','fort','marker',29.927778,-81.325278,'area','public','walk','forts','1738–1763',
  'The governor of Spanish Florida founded Fort Mose in 1738 as a settlement of free Africans, mostly people who had escaped slavery in the Carolinas and Georgia: the first free Black town legally sanctioned in what became the United States. It was abandoned after the Battle of Bloody Mose in 1740, rebuilt nearby and lived in again from 1752 to 1763. Archaeologists found the second fort in 1986; the first has never been located.',
  'Salt marsh on Mose Creek, a visitor center and small museum, and a replica of the 1738 fort that opened in May 2025. Nothing of the fort itself shows above ground.',
  'America\'s first legally free Black town, under the marsh.',
  'Fort Mose Historic State Park (Florida State Parks), 15 Fort Mose Trail. Grounds open daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. and free; the visitor center is open Thursday to Monday, $2 (Fort Mose Historical Society page and visitor bureau listing; the state\'s own page refused the tool). Parking on site.',
  [W('Fort Mose'),['Fort Mose Historical Society: museum','https://fortmose.org/?p=821'],VB('fort-mose','Fort Mose')],th=[29.92929,-81.32532],thn='Fort Mose park lot (OpenStreetMap)',hours='Grounds daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; visitor center Thursday to Monday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.',brick='?'),
N('st-augustine-water-works','St. Augustine Water Works','St. Augustine','fl','water','standing',29.90992,-81.32087,'exact','view','walk','','1896–1898',
  'The pumping station of the city\'s first water utility, built 1896–1898 by Guild & White. A reliable supply brought fire hydrants and the city\'s first paid firemen. In 1928 the water-works park became Davenport Park and the building its clubhouse, later a playhouse and garden centre. The city restored it from 2015 to 2021.',
  'A red brick pumping station with a steep hipped roof; inside, original plaster, beadboard and hex tiles, and the exposed brick of the boiler room with old paint left on it. The music platform outside was a semicircular brick holding tank (city tour page; Register form).',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of St. Augustine and used by the St. Johns Cultural Council, in Davenport Park on San Marco Avenue. The city publishes a self-guided tour; opening hours not checked. '+TOWN,
  [NR('13001134','St. Augustine Water Works'),['City of St. Augustine: Historic Waterworks self-guided tour','https://citystaug.com/1069/Historic-Waterworks-Self-guided-Tour']],brick='yes'),
]

